  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1259 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nA calm and warm weather day is on display at this hour with\ncurrent temperatures in the 80s to lower 90s with a thin cumulus\nshield building from the northwest. Taking a look at satellite\nimagery, a MCV is currently rotating over central Arkansas\nigniting a few pop-up thunderstorms over Little Rock. This MCV\nhas given lift to a few pop-up thunderstorms over northeast\nArkansas and the Missouri Bootheel. Meanwhile, a secondary MCV is\ncurrently just west of Springfield, Missouri. As we head into the\nmid afternoon into early evening hours, CAMs are hinting at this\nsecondary MCV translating southeast and making its way over to\nthe Mid-South. Shower and thunderstorm coverage will continue to\nuptick through the afternoon hours and linger into the early\nmorning hours tomorrow.\n\nDuring peak heating this afternoon, a window will open up for a\nvery conditional chance for severe weather. Digging into the\nparameter spacing, around 1500-2000 J/kg SBCAPE, around 20 kts of\neffective shear, DCAPE upwards of 900, mid-level lapse rates of\naround 6.5 C/km, and steepening low-level lapse rates of around\n8.0 C/km. If the current, overall weak, cap breaks and\nthunderstorms are able to tap into enough instability for tall\nenough updrafts, the primary concern will be damaging winds\nthrough the late evening hours. Best chances will exist north of\nthe I-40 corridor. Showers and thunderstorms are expected to\nlinger through the overnight hours, though severe weather chances\nlook to cut off around midnight as shear and instability wanes.\n\nAs the main shortwave driving the aforementioned MCVs pushes\neast, another wave of isolated to scattered shower and\nthunderstorm chances will exist tomorrow afternoon mainly along\nand south of I-40. Increased cloud cover and lingering rain\nchances will influence high temperatures and overall Heat\nAdvisory potential tomorrow. NBM continues to hold on to 105+F\nheat indices in the Mississippi Delta region, however, HREF and\nLREF have <20% chance. Moving into early next week, heat will\nbecome the main weather story as a sub-tropical upper-level\nridge, currently centered over the four corners region, begins to\nslowly nudge east. Cloud cover and rain chances will begin to\npush past the Mid-South by Sunday, likely warranting a Heat\nAdvisory in at least the Delta Region. Heading into next\nworkweek, ensembles are hinting at a secondary ridge beginning to\nbroaden over the southeastern CONUS. Here in the Mid-South, highs\nwill remain in the low to mid 90s with heat likely peaking on\nWednesday. Heat Advisories across at least portions of our area\nwill be warranted each day. Isolated shower and thunderstorm\nchances will also exist each day next workweek as PWATs remain\naround 1.8\". By the latter half of next week, the overall upper-\nlevel pattern becomes unsettled as we move into more of a\nnorthwest flow regime and rain chances begin to increase once\nagain.\n\nAEH",
